#{[17592186278350 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278350 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160967" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278350 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278350 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/21/21952fe273fd1a1ecb03727f4aa254d4e0ecdab2feaeb1d8c652bc9923404846.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{13\\pi }{12}\\le v\\le \\frac{13\\pi }{9}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the left side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278350 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "019ee70c-3708-5fe3-b8b1-31b6dfb8e888" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278350 #edb/ref 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the left side.\n\nFor the left side, we have $u=3$ and $\\frac{13\\pi }{12}\\le v\\le \\frac{13\\pi }{9}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{13\\pi }{12}\\le v\\le \\frac{13\\pi }{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the third quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{13\\pi }{12}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{13\\pi }{9},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3b8b1c7b2a7e68aee7a685e946a0b248470dbe4c5d4dcdfec9cd22783529345c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278352 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278352 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161028" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278352 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278352 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/aa/aa62dda1abb7bc6bca1b2e6cdf356b52946820912ec6318e5901273a546ee96a.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the bottom side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278352 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e82f4301-a070-5bc8-9a49-2d57b54ecb56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278352 #edb/ref 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the bottom side.\n\nFor the bottom side, we have $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $0\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})),\\,0\\le u\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment that makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis, where one endpoint is the point $O,$ while the other endpoint lies on the circle of radius $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/48/48eb6a022826e8f4e1246201e4ad190fff9bb6afdf7b2146d89010e76478a4bf.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278355 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278355 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161032" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278355 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278355 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/16/16d74107dbbf12749766cd9cea7e38d16e28a59efb997bd224aed73c7830324e.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{10\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\nI. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{10\\pi }{9}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nII. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nIII. The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$ centered at $O.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278355 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4b7f9af3-cd62-5b44-9828-72b23454ff87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278355 #edb/ref 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ and $2\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),\\,2\\le u\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bfe75ef6b4ea9ddd72be6501f95fd89dd1e80fb2aed691c3c22b158514141cc1.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is false.\n\n- When $u=2,$ we obtain the point \n$(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $2.$\n\n- When $u=4,$ we obtain the point \n$(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $4.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $2$ and $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278387 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278387 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161118" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278387 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278387 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/83/8360f06b0d9e9633aa00b779702d386cd403d7fe8ac8a28b51922dca92ddafed.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278387 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dc9dfc88-f183-5e04-83ce-439434807dc2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278387 #edb/ref 1074 "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=0$ and $u=\\sqrt{3},$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ and $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/83/832658a3d3a6add5f39be27844d2df16a07576ac8578b03b69ebac784b5b4c61.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389077 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389077 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160909" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389077 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389077 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/95/951c3d3bca4116deebccbbabb5d7405bb07669cacb83c1e450204bb40c247d5c.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389077 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "31d74e75-fb66-5eb8-9774-1cd2896eeaa8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389077 #edb/ref 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{2\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{2}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=2$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=2$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=\\sqrt{2}$ and $r=2,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/20/20f36ce6a4a889d6906f11dff0c4f0ffb5f6e55a857c29f5e4491f0f098b658f.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389078 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389078 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389078 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389078 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389078 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389078 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389078 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389078 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186389080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389078 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "79e6b72a-de31-55a4-8613-ca44de670c50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389079 #edb/ref 1080 "images/82/82557cc5a33963a7ec6d7910da8dc1409fd2cdc1245b00be009f7a237bee6c3a.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389079 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389079 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3f97954b-fab9-555e-a63d-0b7ff6619a7e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389080 #edb/ref 1080 "images/20/20f36ce6a4a889d6906f11dff0c4f0ffb5f6e55a857c29f5e4491f0f098b658f.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389080 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389080 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4f425ff2-e50f-5b19-ab10-931b2a0a25ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389081 #edb/ref 1080 "images/12/128955fddc503b24eb73c5a321a803a4318f16e83e88f1d28be0a0e621f926c9.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389081 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389081 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "35760d42-f2ba-5032-8da1-1ca1d76889e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389082 #edb/ref 1080 "images/1b/1b2026b5c41f64f72aa9d1c849f9357f4192860545e9a41241c2a0f87d33db6a.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389082 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389082 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fde379d9-5306-507a-a69f-f50ff3931c5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389083 #edb/ref 1080 "images/70/709f9c571b81383cb436570ea4c63575a2fb7e83b1c2e377a6347f54c8ceea35.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389083 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389083 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a8a07a20-8567-5538-9f06-4d6de140aa40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389098 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389098 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160944" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389098 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389098 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6e/6e0e9b7d2cbff011a5ff209f335fb3d5e86af02a58c4ea5c8a8d46bd384e6a0c.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the right side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389098 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9c0c66a8-0dcc-5684-892c-2aa4d891f439" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389098 #edb/ref 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the right side.\n\nFor the right side, we have $u=\\sqrt{3}$ and $0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/20/2051bbb1a83dca354f57ff21a1e7208d9acbd9b4122ba8e7406933b449199b52.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389099 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389099 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389099 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389099 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389099 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389099 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389099 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389099 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186389102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389099 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "62e79051-6fca-5a52-820a-2af4080980ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389100 #edb/ref 1080 "images/df/df47e830c98a8f5e3ddfdf909ec23163735b21486eb25d784bb6b88ea95e4f60.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389100 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389100 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "be52c672-2de5-56f0-9576-cf2f1cb2c3c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389101 #edb/ref 1080 "images/77/772ffafe5ebbe9aad24464f1a4ee7937c2a14895f62d6a73e0f1422a0bc899ee.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389101 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389101 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "282b979d-0b3b-5d2d-b38c-80725bf830b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389102 #edb/ref 1080 "images/20/2051bbb1a83dca354f57ff21a1e7208d9acbd9b4122ba8e7406933b449199b52.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389102 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389102 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4892dc8f-6ac8-516f-82fe-ebb19d4650d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389103 #edb/ref 1080 "The image is the empty set" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389103 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389103 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3206686d-f296-5283-8260-05891a5656e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389104 #edb/ref 1080 "images/e8/e8ed422a10846fc3f44fb894d1051f184dde24b5a38181f25366ba209c5986c5.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389104 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389104 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fbbd466a-6afa-5c79-99ef-7a3166b990c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389146 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389147 #ed