 [17592186423493 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186423456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423493 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cb72ef15-e2d9-5b72-84ec-e98850448e17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423494 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423494 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423494 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423494 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423494 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423494 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423494 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423494 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186423457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423494 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d62a4acb-ad20-5c4f-b6b4-f3aea150ce3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423495 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423495 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423495 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423495 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423495 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423495 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423495 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423495 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186423464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423495 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f7528fa6-219c-52b3-82fe-4826326f572d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423496 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423496 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160923" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423496 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423496 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f3/f370177dc8119044b74fd4923c3d4318d5b9e1b4de89b63967284ee1cd7794da.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423496 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "22e295c4-59c2-57da-8c38-5051b16dc6ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423496 #edb/ref 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{2}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=\\sqrt{2}$ and $r=3,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/03/03dae0fbacc0339613580414c2217652b9ff6303788c7ca265deb3b1f755f4cd.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423497 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423497 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161024" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423497 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423497 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a4/a44852c606e67842a58ad1a696253e6e211816f8243beec8b3eea97ef42055a2.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{9}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423497 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e5df847-265a-5b9c-9720-3136b8ccb366" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423497 #edb/ref 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{9}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{9}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{9}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{9})),\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment that makes an angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{9}$ with the positive $x$-axis and whose endpoints lie on circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c5ef04b3fa4765bbad1bd7345fe5217feac73c21251b99426c17ed22ec3bbd1.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423498 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423498 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161159" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423498 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423498 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/38/383b3750be20f665159215eea6b0134c0da930f574e7ef12ff7127b78ac22acb.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423498 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9b6d5f3b-6c7c-587f-b086-0bc40be20551" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423498 #edb/ref 1074 "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $u=3,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}$ and $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{8},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/5e/5eb48411cf0c9efd97bc99a4c5f6edd064664c98eeef87ceda6d9909a5056eab.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423499 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423499 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160947" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423499 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423499 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/16/1686ee5f02ab2215b700216f8dd0844d4763d0f3bde9837cb7913d2790a9c310.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423499 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b54a0f96-7cef-5900-9667-b793d1ee0611" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423499 #edb/ref 1074 "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\frac{3}{2},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=1$ and $u=\\frac{3}{2},$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b8/b82293b9940cff262ec5601f8652df8aa739fb7e3d1cf66a323cff4d1c46ff0f.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423500 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423500 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160968" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423500 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423500 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/93/93132c9352ed5068001c824741a1c0a7cb8dca82564ad91db706bc1ecfcedbcc.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 6,\\,\\,\\frac{14\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the right side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423500 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b5a18f4e-2d75-5904-b5e5-ab7ba2838493" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423500 #edb/ref 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the right side.\n\nFor the right side, we have $u=6$ and $\\frac{14\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=6$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{14\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $6$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the fourth quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{14\\pi }{9}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/b6/b6052c625de5a1d392975afae8c1053b2b430859ba6fbc3bc8c2c3de01d40318.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423501 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423501 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161029" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423501 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423501 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/12/1252844f7a9e2fb2ca0ff1d2e2e1546a460e7d0846b4ae2996b6ce984ee305a1.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\le u\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\nI. The image makes the angle of $0$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nII. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nIII. The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $1$ centered at $O.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423501 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fa0b27c3-d4dd-5c0c-8989-66af8729df06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423501 #edb/ref 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ and $\\frac{1}{4}\\le u\\le 1.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})),\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/49/49aa7d6ccd4b14b48ede7486f41ab11ac22b593c06d75795fe7819889b2b2f23.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is true.\n\n- When $u=\\frac{1}{4},$ we obtain the point \n$(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}),\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\n- When $u=1,$ we obtain the point \n$(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $1.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423502 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423502 #edb/ref 1069 "q-160924" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423502 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423502 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/19/19f21fa7abb989e8c2bf35552675f498969e30e1f42012fd615c00686d20cb01.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{6\\pi }{7}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423502 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ff752515-0aa8-5668-9f6f-2eed5f67dc87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423502 #edb/ref 1074 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ and $1\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{6\\pi }{7}$ and $1\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{6\\pi }{7}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{7}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{7})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{6\\pi }{7}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{6\\pi }{7}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{7}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $\\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{6\\pi }{7}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{7}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=3,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{7},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/50/50462754e89259447fe464f3a9c8ca7f165260d4b7adfc25585912291091838b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
